✅ 20. Synchronize on a Custom Lock Object

20.1 Definition

Instead of synchronizing on
this
or another publicly accessible object,
we create a dedicated private lock object.

private final Object lock = new Object();
Then synchronize on it.

synchronized(lock){

}

This is called using a custom lock object.

20.2 Why Do We Need a Custom Lock?

Suppose we write
synchronized(this){

}

Anyone with a reference to our object can do
synchronized(counter){

}

Now our internal synchronization is affected by external code.
We lose control.

Instead
private final Object lock = new Object();
Nobody outside the class can access it.

Now
only our class controls the lock.

20.3 Syntax

private final Object lock = new Object();
public void increment(){
    synchronized(lock){
    }
}

20.4 Internal Working

Suppose Thread-A enters
increment();

Flow

Acquire lock monitor
↓
Execute Critical Section
↓
Release lock monitor

Meanwhile Thread-B

Acquire lock monitor
↓
Already Locked
↓
BLOCKED

